.TH VIF 6 "" vif
.SH NAME
vif \- a modal\-motion arcade game
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vif
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without flags,
.B vif
starts a solo game in the terminal, or in a desktop window when a vif-gui build
or the window key of vif.toml's [presentation] asks for one.
The flags below also host, join or serve a networked session,
replay or script a run, play a bot, and validate the installed configuration.
.PP
Each resource resolves from its own flag, then
.BR \-config\-dir ,
then the user configuration root, then the
.I wad
of the checkout whose
.I bin
holds the binary, then each system root,
and finally the copy compiled into the binary.
The first root holding a
.I vif.toml
supplies the defaults of the path flags, the audio buffer, the desktop window and
the [play] settings; a flag given on the command line wins.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Session
.TP
.B \-host <doors>
Play at once and open the game to participants at one or more doors, comma\-separated: :7777 for tcp, ws://:7777 or wss://:7777 for WebSocket
.TP
.B \-serve <doors>
Bind a headless session with no local cursor at one or more doors, e.g. :7777 or :7777,wss://:7778
.TP
.B \-cert <file>
Certificate chain a wss:// door serves, PEM; for \-host, \-serve or :host
.TP
.B \-key <file>
The certificate's private key, PEM; default the \-cert file
.TP
.B \-selfsign
Serve wss:// with a certificate made for this run, in place of \-cert: encrypted, and taken as it is by a terminal that joins
.TP
.B \-passphrase <phrase|new>
Doors take this passphrase (Argon2\-SCRAM), or one made now and printed; with \-join, the one shown to a door that asks
.TP
.B \-admit <open|ticket:key>
What a \-host or \-serve door admits beside a pass: open, or the site's tickets under the router's public key(s), comma\-separated, and no bare key
.TP
.B \-ca <file|sha256:hex>
What a wss:// host's certificate is trusted by when joining, beyond the system's roots and a self\-signed one taken as it is: a PEM file of its issuer, or the certificate's own fingerprint
.TP
.B \-join <addr>
Join a session at [tcp://|vif://|vifs://]host:port[/name] or a ws(s):// URL, or a new one an http(s):// site creates; tcp when no scheme is given
.TP
.B \-spectate <addr>
Spectate a session at an address \-join takes: watch it from one of its players, taking no place in it; a slot after the address names the player looked from first
.TP
.B \-spectators <n>
How many spectators a session this run hosts feeds at once, 0..1024; 0 takes none (default 8)
.TP
.B \-name <name>
Name this host answers to, so one address can serve several sessions
.TP
.B \-players <n>
Roster ceiling including self, 2..16 (with \-serve, guests only, 1..16); unset holds the whole roster; with a \-join site, the one requested
.TP
.B \-bot [N[:graph]|graph]
Add bots alongside you (default 1:default); \-host/\-join keep your player
.TP
.B \-authority host|migrate
What a session does when its host goes: host ends it, the default; migrate moves it to a guest that allowed taking over, whose address the session shares
.TP
.B \-slow\-window <dur>
Window a participant's lateness is judged over; host only, 0 never evicts (default 20s)
.TP
.B \-slow\-late <n>
Evict at this many late epochs per second of game time over the window; 0 ignores lateness (default 5)
.TP
.B \-slow\-bytes <n>
\&...and at least this many bytes per second of it on its link; 0 ignores throughput (default 8192)
.TP
.B \-shed\-window <dur>
Window the host's own tick keeping is judged over; past 10% of its ticks dropped, it drops its newest bot, then player; host only, 0 never sheds (default 10s)
.TP
.B \-listen <addr>
Listen here so this run can take a migrate session over, whatever [play] takeover says; \-join only, and the session shares the address
.TP
.B \-relay <addr>
Admit participants that cannot reach the host here and carry their traffic, or at a door published through vif://<router>; \-join only
.TP
.B \-size <WxH>
Terminal\-equivalent size for a run that has no terminal
.TP
.B \-probe <addr>
Health and metrics endpoint; \-serve only
.TP
.B \-first\-join <dur>
Exit if no guest connects within this; \-serve only, 0 waits forever
.TP
.B \-empty <dur>
Exit this long after the last guest leaves; \-serve only, 0 keeps the session
.TP
.B \-drain <dur>
How long a termination signal waits for an empty roster; \-serve only, 0 exits at once
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B \-d, \-config\-embedded
Use the embedded scenario and content; refuses \-s and \-f and ignores vif.toml's scenario and content
.TP
.B \-config\-dir <dir>
Configuration root holding scenario/ input/ audio/ content/ image/ bot/
.TP
.B \-s, \-config\-scenario <name|path>
Installed scenario name or path below scenario/, scenario.toml, or a scenario directory
.TP
.B \-f, \-config\-content <path>
Content directory, or a single content file
.TP
.B \-k, \-config\-keymap <path>
Keymap TOML
.TP
.B \-config\-music <path>
Music pattern override TOML
.TP
.B \-config\-sounds <path>
Sound definition override TOML
.SS Presentation and audio
.TP
.B \-color auto|256|true
Colour depth; auto detects the terminal
.TP
.B \-g, \-gui[=false]
Open the game in a desktop window (Xlib, EGL, GLES 2 at run time; Linux, FreeBSD) instead of the terminal; the default of a vif\-gui build or with vif.toml's [presentation] window = true, which \-g=false turns off
.TP
.B \-cli
Play in the terminal whatever vif.toml's [presentation] or a vif\-gui build says; wins over \-g
.TP
.B \-maximized[=false]
Open the desktop window maximized; vif.toml's [presentation] maximized is the default, and a terminal run ignores it
.TP
.B \-fullscreen[=false]
Open the desktop window full screen; vif.toml's [presentation] fullscreen is the default, and a terminal run ignores it
.TP
.B \-scale <n>
Draw the desktop window's font n times its pixels, for a HiDPI screen; with \-g (default 1)
.TP
.B \-mute[=false]
Start muted, or with sound as \-mute=false; unset, vif.toml's [play] channels stand, sound on by default
.TP
.B \-ab, \-audio\-backend <name>
Force an audio backend instead of detecting one
.SS Run
.TP
.B \-nw, \-no\-wait[=false]
Start each new game and seat each join at once, or at a key, click or tap as \-nw=false; unset, vif.toml's [play] wait stands, waiting by default
.TP
.B \-seed <n>
Root RNG seed; 0 draws one and logs it
.TP
.B \-speed <rate>
Simulation rate 1/8 1/4 1/2 1 2 4 8; with \-script or autonomous bots also "max" for no wall pacing
.TP
.B \-tick <dur>
Game time one tick advances, 10ms to 100ms; a host's is its session's, which joiners take (default 50ms)
.TP
.B \-script <path>
Run an authored deterministic TOML tick script
.TP
.B \-headless
With \-bot, run bots without a human player or terminal; solo runs are unpaced. With \-replay, verify the journal's digests unattended
.TP
.B \-watch
Watch the first bot or a script on this terminal, with no human player
.TP
.B \-r, \-replay <path>
Replay a recorded journal instead of playing
.TP
.B \-check
Validate the resolved settings, scenario, keymap, audio, content and bot graphs, then exit
.TP
.B \-schema
Print the FSM schema as JSON, then exit
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.B \-l, \-log[=DIR]
Enable logging; DIR overrides $XDG_STATE_HOME/vif/log
.TP
.B \-lv, \-log\-level <level>
trace, debug, info, warn or error; implies \-l
.TP
.B \-ls, \-log\-scope <spec>
Which subsystems log; see Scopes below; implies \-l
.TP
.B \-lt, \-log\-stat <ticks>
Status snapshot period in game ticks, 0 disables; implies \-l
.TP
.B \-lr, \-log\-recorder <ticks>
Flight recorder depth in game ticks, 0 disables; implies \-l
.TP
.B \-log\-session\-id <id>
Attach a session ID to every application log record; implies \-l
.TP
.B \-log\-stdout
Write the log to stdout as JSON instead of to a file; implies \-l
.TP
.B \-j, \-journal[=DIR]
Record a replay journal; DIR overrides $XDG_STATE_HOME/vif/journal
.TP
.B \-mw, \-music\-wav[=DIR]
Record the music alone, as it plays, to a WAV file; DIR overrides $XDG_STATE_HOME/vif/music
.TP
.B \-dev[=false]
Capture runtime stderr to a file; on by default for \-race builds
.SS Help
.TP
.B \-h, \-help
Print this and exit
.TP
.B \-version
Print the module version and commit a package should report
.SS Scopes (\-ls)
.nf
  Names     app (a)  fsm (f)  event (e)  dispatch (d)  push (p)  input (i)
            stat (s)  rec (r)  lock (l)  net (n)  session (m)  converge (c)
            journal (j)  domain (o)  tap (t)
  Sets      all is every scope, none is nothing
  Combine   join names or letters with +, a comma or a space \[u2014] "app+fsm+stat" and "afs" are one set
  Adjust    lead with + or \- to add to or remove from the set already selected
.fi
.SH IN-GAME MENU
Ctrl-G or F10, Escape in normal mode, :menu (:g, :config) or a press held on the
status bar opens the menu, and F1 or :help the help. Select a page or row with
Up/Down or j/k; section titles are skipped. Left/Right or h/l changes values;
Enter/Space chooses. A press
selects a row, and on the selected row acts as Enter. / searches every page: the
rows matching all the words typed list with their page and act in place; Enter
keeps the search and Escape clears it. Escape or q returns to the pages, then
closes; Ctrl-G or F10 closes from any page. Home/End and PageUp/PageDown navigate
longer pages. A scenario is the set of regions that defines a game; a game is a
scenario running. On Android the app draws the same menu with its own widgets:
pages expand in place, settings are switches, radio lists and \- and + steppers,
a destructive action confirms first, Back goes back a level, and the keys above
work from a keyboard.
.PP
Game pauses the game until Resume, as :pause (:p) or p does, restarts it from its
scenario's first tick, as :n does, and sets its speed; in a session only the host
can, and every participant follows at the same tick. A replay adds its restart.
.PP
Scenarios lists the scenarios the configuration roots hold, each by its name,
or by its path below scenario/ where the name reaches another, the running one
marked current: Enter or a press marks one, and Start starts a game of it. Other
scenario is a text box on its row for a name or path the list does not hold. In
a session only the host can, and its participants rejoin.
.PP
Multiplayer hosts this game, joins or spectates an address or link typed in its
row's text box, requests a session from an HTTP(S) vif-router site with a player
count and scenario, leaves the session, relays for others, and makes the games
this run hosts visible on its local network, as :lan on does; Nearby games lists
the visible games found there, each joined from its row. Player count 0 and a blank
scenario use server defaults. Current session status heads the page. A session
ends when its host leaves, each player going on alone, unless the host chose
migrate: \-authority migrate, the Authority field, or :host <address> migrate.
A migrate session moves to a guest that turned Take over as host on, as
:takeover on does; such a guest listens on a port in each session it joins, and
the session shares its address with the other players. Hosts drop
a player with its bots, or one bot, under Players; :player lists slots and
:player drop <slot> removes one and keeps it out. Bots adds local bots by count and policy graph,
and removes this run's bots by cursor slot or cancels a joining bot; :bot drop
<slot> removes your bot, or any bot when hosting. Slots accept decimal 10,
hexadecimal A or 0xA for the cursor marked A. Host before adding bots to solo
play if remote players should join; otherwise bots open a session only they can
join. Forms use Up/Down between fields, Left/Right and Home/End to move the text
cursor, Backspace/Delete to edit, Enter to submit, and Escape to cancel.
Successful submissions resume play; asynchronous progress and failures appear in
the status bar. A failed join preserves the current game.
.PP
A spectator watches a session and takes no place in it: \-spectate, :spectate
<address> [slot] or the Spectate a session row replaces this run, its bots and
its journal with one that only watches. It looks from one player's cursor, whose
slot the status bar names: the keys that move left or up follow the player
before, right or down the one after, a press on a player's cursor follows that
one, and :spectate <slot> or its row under Players names one. When that player
leaves, the view moves to the next one playing. Nothing else it types plays;
:leave starts a local game. Players are not told who watches. A host feeds
\-spectators spectators at once, and :session counts them.
.PP
Settings holds sound effects, their volume and music; auto-fire, mouse input and
pointer following; the frame cap and the telemetry HUD; whether the menu and
other windows, or the command line, pause the game; and whether a game waits for
its first press. Developer controls logging, the flight recorder and the runtime
profiler, captures profiles, an execution trace and a telemetry snapshot, clears
HUD pins, shows the navigation overlays and records a replay journal. Values in
brackets are unavailable or read only; the selected row explains why. Launch
options explains the file or flag that sets what a running game cannot change.
.PP
Settings, Relay for others, Take over as host and Visible on this network are
vif.toml's [play]: a run writes each back as it changes, so the next run starts as
this one was left:
to the vif.toml it read when that is in the user root or the \-config-dir root,
else to the user root's, created at the first change from the one read. Every
other change lasts the run.
A game that can pause, solo or a host whose only other participants are its own
bots, pauses while the menu is open unless Pause in menu is off, and while the
command line is open unless Pause in command line is off; the menu's title says
whether the game is paused or running. Any other session keeps running. The menu
shares the command line's replay and session restrictions. The keymap binds
config_menu and help in normal_keys, text_keys and overlay_keys, and pause in
normal; a key names one action, and an action can have several keys.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vif
User configuration root, normally
.IR ~/.config/vif .
.TP
.I vif.toml
Settings at the top of a configuration root: another root, the log, journal,
music, scenario, content, keymap and downloads paths, the audio mixer buffer,
whether the desktop window opens and how, the zoom Android and a browser keep, the
[play] settings the menu writes back, and what a run keeps of its sessions.
.TP
.I scenario/
Scenarios, each a directory holding scenario.toml, in groups to any depth:
base/ holds the shipped ones, downloads/ the scenarios sessions' hosts sent
when [session] save_downloads saves them, and a player files their own as they
like.
.TP
.I bot/<name>.toml
Bot graphs that \-bot and :bot add name; a path also works. The default
graph is compiled in and resolves last.
.TP
.IR /usr/local/etc/vif ", " /etc/vif
System configuration roots, in that order;
.B VIF_SYSTEM_ROOTS
names others in their place, separated by colons.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/vif
Logs, journals, music recordings and the places a run comes back to its sessions
with, normally
.IR ~/.local/state/vif .
